================================================================================
TEST T44296 -- (259) Management_LLDP - LLDP
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-21 14:44-15:39 NZDT.  LLDP was measured on the wire on an
          AT-x960-56YSQ, then removed and restored.
          The DUT's clock reads UTC and is 15 s behind the box's UTC.  Device times
          below are UTC, and tool and console times are box local (NZDT).
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2 = swi_d (DUT), u3 = swi_b, u5 = swi_a;
          Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     T44296.  The test-case database holds NO authored objective, NO step text, NO
          test data and NO expected result for this case.  Its step list is empty.
          The only authored authority is the case title.
          The tester designed the objective and all 12 steps below from three sources:
          the case title; the X960 TPS, row 257 "LLDP = Yes"; and the DUT's own CLI
          surface, read read-only with `<cmd> ?` before anything was sent.
          Objective (designed by the tester), in six parts.
          1. The DUT sends LLDP on the wire at the rate and with the TTL it is configured
             for.
          2. The DUT receives LLDP and builds a neighbour entry from the fields in the
             frame.
          3. `lldp transmit` and `lldp receive` each control one direction, and nothing
             else.
          4. A neighbour ages out on the TTL the neighbour advertised, and an LLDP
             shutdown frame removes it at once.
          5. `lldp tlv-select`, `lldp timer`, `lldp holdtime-multiplier` and the LLDP SNMP
             notification all change what leaves the device, and all can be removed again.
          6. LLDP runs on a port that spanning tree is blocking, and an LLDP configuration
             survives a reboot.
          Every verdict below is graded on frames on the wire, or on the DUT's own
          per-port LLDP frame counters.  `show lldp` output is quoted as corroboration
          only, never as the proof.
