VERDICT: PASS -- LLDP works on the AT-x960-56YSQ, and every limb was proven with frames.
      transmission on the wire ......... yes      (30.03 s between frames, three times, TTL 120 in every frame)
      configured rate = measured rate .. yes      (`Tx Timer Interval 30 secs` -> 30.03 s measured)
      configured TTL = TTL on wire ..... yes      (multiplier 4 x 30 s = `Computed TTL 120 secs` -> TTL TLV 120 s)
      reception, field by field ........ yes      (chassis ID, port ID, system name and capabilities all came back unchanged)
      frame accounting is exact ........ yes      (the `In` counter moved by +3, +0, +3, +2, +1 for 3, 3, 3, 2 and 1 frames sent)
      transmit and receive independent . yes      (all four on/off combinations measured; see the table in STEP 4)
      ageing uses the neighbour's TTL .. yes      (TTL 20 entry gone inside an 8 s window; a TTL 120 entry on the same port stayed)
      shutdown frame removes at once ... yes      (entry deleted with about 110 s of hold time unused)
      the two removals are counted apart yes      (age-out moves two counters; a shutdown frame moves only `Deleted Entries`)
      `lldp tlv-select` changes the wire yes      (4 TLVs -> 11 TLVs, every value cross-checked; removal -> 4 TLVs again)
      timers change the wire ........... yes      (10.01 s between frames, TTL 20; then back to 30.03 s, TTL 120)
      SNMP notifications are real ...... yes      (3 lldpRemTablesChange traps caught for 3 changes; 0 traps for 2 changes with notification off)
      `no lldp run` stops both ways .... yes      (0 frames sent in 70 s, and 0 of 3 frames received or counted)
      LLDP runs on RSTP-blocked ports .. yes      (3 Discarding ports moved as many frames as the Forwarding one; a no-link port moved 0)
      configuration survives a reboot .. yes      (15 s timer, 45 s TTL and a System Name TLV all measured after a cold boot)
