STEP 1 -- the DUT transmits LLDP at the configured rate and TTL                PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: capture LLDP on the testbox leg with the bench untouched, then decode every
  TLV.  Nothing was configured first.  A 75 s capture is long enough to hold three frames
  at the configured 30 s rate, so the rate itself can be measured and not assumed.
  Capture on the box: tools/tb capture tb277 lldp1159 eth3 75 s1_on.pcap ether proto 0x88cc

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/s1_on.pcap --tlv
    pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/s1_on.pcap
    packets   3 total, 3 LLDP

    source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
      frames 3 over 60.1 s
      inter-frame gap  min 30.03 s  mean 30.03 s  max 30.03 s
      vlan tags        untagged
      --- frame at 1792547785.844, 4 TLVs ---
        Chassis ID                         MAC address: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
        Port ID                            Interface name: port1.0.10
        Time To Live                       120 s
        OUI e01aea subtype 2               0c26573a87c541829d436cb04a0e77c2
      --- frame at 1792547815.873, 4 TLVs ---   (identical TLVs)
      --- frame at 1792547845.903, 4 TLVs ---   (identical TLVs)

  <-- The DUT sends to 01:80:c2:00:00:0e, the IEEE 802.1AB group address.
  <-- The gap is 30.03 s three times, with no spread.  `show lldp` reads
      `Tx Timer Interval 30 secs`.  The configured rate is the rate on the wire.
  <-- The TTL TLV reads 120 s.  `show lldp` reads `Hold-time Multiplier 4` and
      `(Computed TTL value 120 secs)`.  30 x 4 = 120, measured on the wire.
  <-- The chassis ID e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 is the DUT's own bridge MAC, the same value that
      `show spanning-tree brief` prints as `Bridge Id 8000:e01aead47043`.
  <-- The port ID is the sending port's own name, port1.0.10.  That is what
      `Port Number Type ... Port-Number` means.
  <-- The frame's SOURCE MAC is e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43, not the chassis MAC.  It is the
      chassis MAC with the locally-administered bit set (e0 becomes e2).  A capture
      filter written on the chassis address finds nothing.

  Finding: by default this platform advertises only the three mandatory TLVs.  Every
  frame carries Chassis ID, Port ID and Time To Live, which IEEE 802.1AB makes
  mandatory, plus one Allied Telesis organisationally-specific TLV (OUI e0:1a:ea,
  subtype 2, 16 bytes, the same value in every frame).  There is no System Name, System
  Description, System Capabilities, Management Address or Port Description TLV.

awplus#show lldp interface port1.0.10
     Port     Rx/Tx  Notif  Management Addr  Base       802.1    802.3    MED
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     1.0.10   RxTx   -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- McNpLoPe--

  <-- The configuration agrees.  Every Base, 802.1 and 802.3 optional TLV is off.
  <-- This explains the empty `Neighbor Sys Name` column in STEP 0.  No switch here
      advertises a System Name TLV by default, so there is nothing for that column to
      show.  The partners are not failing to identify themselves.
  => STEP 1 PASS: the DUT really sends LLDP.  The rate on the wire is 30.03 s, which is
     the configured 30 s.  The TTL in the frame is 120 s, which is the configured
     multiplier 4 times the 30 s timer.  Both configured numbers were read back out of
     captured frames, not out of a show command.

STEP 9 -- LLDP runs on ports that spanning tree is blocking                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: LLDP is a link-local protocol.  It should run on a link whether or not
  spanning tree lets that link forward data.  RSTP leaves three of swi_d's inter-switch
  ports Discarding and one Forwarding (STEP 0), so the two cases can be compared in one
  read.  `show lldp neighbors` would prove nothing here, so the step is graded on the
  DUT's own per-port frame counters.  port1.0.20 has no link at all and is read in the
  same command as the zero control.
  Measured in the same window as STEP 1.

awplus#show lldp statistics interface port1.0.2,port1.0.4,port1.0.10,port1.0.14,port1.0.16,port1.0.20
  read at DUT 02:06:44 UTC and again at 02:08:26 UTC, a 101 s window:

    port        RSTP role / state                  Out delta   In delta
    port1.0.2   Rootport  / Forwarding  (swi_b)        +3         +3
    port1.0.4   Alternate / Discarding  (swi_a)        +3         +3
    port1.0.14  Alternate / Discarding  (swi_a)        +3         +4
    port1.0.16  Alternate / Discarding  (swi_b)        +3         +3
    port1.0.10  Designated/ Forwarding  (testbox)      +3         +0
    port1.0.20  Disabled  / Discarding, NO LINK        +0         +0

  <-- The three Discarding ports moved as many frames as the Forwarding one: +3 out and
      +3 or +4 in over 101 s.  That is one frame every 30 s in each direction, which is
      the configured Tx Timer.
  <-- port1.0.20 counted zero in both directions in the same read.  The counter is
      therefore discriminating.  A non-zero on a blocked port is real traffic and not an
      artefact.
  <-- port1.0.10 counted In +0.  Nothing but this case's own crafted frames ever sends
      LLDP on the testbox leg.  So every later In increment on port1.0.10 comes from
      tools/lldpsend.py and from nothing else.
  <-- The counter agrees with the wire.  port1.0.10 sent +3 frames in 101 s, and STEP 1
      measured 30.03 s between frames.  101 / 30 = 3.
  => STEP 9 PASS: LLDP transmits and receives normally on an RSTP-blocked port.  The DUT
     also holds neighbour entries on links that carry no data: swi_a is a neighbour on
     port1.0.4 and port1.0.14, and both are Discarding.  A port with no link counted
     zero in the same read, which proves the measurement discriminates.

