STEP 4 -- `no lldp receive` stops reception, and only reception                PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the mirror image of STEP 3.  Remove receive, leave transmit on, and show that
  the two commands are genuinely independent and not one switch with two names.

awplus(config-if)#no lldp receive        (on interface port1.0.10, transmit left ON)
awplus#show lldp interface port1.0.10
     1.0.10   -- Tx  -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- McNpLoPe--

  <-- `-- Tx` means receive off, transmit on.  It is the exact inverse of STEP 3.

  Three crafted frames were sent, identical to STEP 2's:

awplus#show lldp statistics interface port1.0.10
                           before      after 3 frames
      Frames:    In          7              7        (+0)
      Neighbors: New Entries 2              2        (+0)
      show lldp neighbors:   total 4        total 4

  <-- The frames were not merely ignored for the table.  They were not counted at all.
      On this platform `no lldp receive` drops the frame before the packet counter.  In
      STEP 2 three frames moved In by exactly three.
  <-- Consequence worth knowing: the In counter counts frames ACCEPTED, not frames
      ARRIVED.  It cannot be used to prove a frame reached a port.

tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/s4_rxoff.pcap    (a 70 s capture, receive still off)
    source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
      frames 2 over 30.0 s
      inter-frame gap  min 30.03 s  mean 30.03 s  max 30.03 s

  <-- The control holds.  The DUT kept transmitting at its configured rate on the very
      port whose reception was disabled.

  The configuration was then removed and the arc closed.  `lldp receive` restored gives
  `Rx Tx`; three more crafted frames moved In from 7 to 10 (+3) and New Entries from 2 to
  3 (+1), and the neighbour came back:
awplus#show lldp neighbors
    Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 5
    1.0.10   000d.b955.770e  ckLLDP-eth3     cktb277-host            -------S

  All four on/off combinations, measured:

    port1.0.10 config     DUT frames out (capture)    host frames in (In counter)
    Rx Tx (default)       2 in 70 s at 30.03 s        +3 for 3 sent
    Rx -- (no transmit)   0 in 75 s                   +3 for 3 sent
    -- Tx (no receive)    2 in 70 s at 30.03 s        +0 for 3 sent
    Rx Tx (restored)      2 in 70 s at 30.03 s        +3 for 3 sent

  => STEP 4 PASS: the two commands are independent.  Each controls one direction and
     leaves the other direction working.  The table above shows all four combinations,
     and each was measured with frames.

STEP 5 -- a neighbour ages out on the TTL the NEIGHBOUR advertised             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the rule is that the receiver holds an entry for the TTL carried in the frame.
  With only switch partners this means waiting 120 s with nothing to compare against.
  The testbox lets the TTL be chosen, so two neighbours with DIFFERENT TTLs were put on
  the SAME port at the SAME time.  One must go and the other must stay.  That pair is
  the measurement.  Neither neighbour alone would prove anything.

  The first neighbour, cktb277-host, was already live with TTL 120.  A second one was
  sent with TTL 20:

tb277$ tools/tb sudotool tb277 lldp1159 lldpsend eth3 --count 2 --interval 1 --ttl 20 \
         --chassis-mac 02:00:00:00:00:aa --src-mac 02:00:00:00:00:aa \
         --port-id ckAGE-ttl20 --sys-name ckage20 --caps station
      1792549055.766 sent frame 1/2          = 15:17:35 NZDT
      1792549056.806 sent frame 2/2          = 15:17:36 NZDT   <- T0, the last frame

  `show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10` was then read about every 8 s:

    box local   since T0   Total neighbors   entries
    15:17:42      +6 s           2           ckLLDP-eth3 (TTL 120) and ckAGE-ttl20 (TTL 20)
    15:17:51     +15 s           2           both still present
    15:17:59     +23 s           1           ckAGE-ttl20 is GONE; ckLLDP-eth3 remains
    15:18:07     +31 s           1           ckLLDP-eth3 only
    15:18:15     +39 s           1           ckLLDP-eth3 only

  The two reads that bracket the expiry, verbatim:

awplus#show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10          (15:17:51)
    Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 2
    1.0.10   000d.b955.770e  ckLLDP-eth3     cktb277-host            -------S
    1.0.10   0200.0000.00aa  ckAGE-ttl20     ckage20                 -------S

awplus#show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10          (15:17:59)
    Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 1
    1.0.10   000d.b955.770e  ckLLDP-eth3     cktb277-host            -------S

awplus#show lldp statistics interface port1.0.10
      Neighbors: Deleted Entries ....... 3      (was 2, so +1)
                 Entry Age-outs ........ 3      (was 2, so +1)

  <-- TTL 20 counted from T0 expires at 15:17:56.  The entry was present at +15 s and
      gone at +23 s.  The expiry is bracketed to an 8 s window around the predicted
      instant.  The polling interval sets that precision, not the device.
  <-- The TTL used is the neighbour's, not a local constant.  The DUT's own
      `Computed TTL value` stayed at 120 s throughout, and the TTL-120 neighbour on the
      SAME port was untouched while the TTL-20 one expired.  One port, one instant, two
      outcomes, and the only difference is the TTL inside the frame.
  <-- `Entry Age-outs` incremented, which names the mechanism.  STEP 6 removes an entry
      a different way and that counter does not move.
  => STEP 5 PASS: the DUT held the entry for the TTL the sender advertised and no
     longer.  A second entry with a different TTL on the same port survived, which rules
     out a local timer or a table-wide flush.

STEP 6 -- an LLDP shutdown frame (TTL 0) removes the entry at once             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: IEEE 802.1AB says a station that is leaving sends an LLDPDU with TTL 0, so the
  neighbour drops it immediately instead of waiting out the hold time.  No show command
  can see this.  It is only testable with a sender under our control.  cktb277-host was
  live with TTL 120 and had about 110 s of hold time left.  One frame was sent, carrying
  TTL 0 and nothing else changed.

tb277$ tools/tb sudotool tb277 lldp1159 lldpsend eth3 --count 0 --shutdown --ttl 120 \
         --port-id ckLLDP-eth3 --sys-name cktb277-host --caps station --mgmt-ip 10.38.22.33
      1792549120.894 sent SHUTDOWN frame (TTL 0)        = 15:18:40 NZDT

awplus#show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10          (15:18:48, 8 s later)
    Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 0

awplus#show lldp statistics interface port1.0.10         (15:18:49)
      Frames:    In .................... 13       (was 10, so +3: 2 from STEP 5 plus this one)
      Neighbors: New Entries ........... 4
                 Deleted Entries ....... 4        (was 3, so +1)
                 Entry Age-outs ........ 3        (was 3, so +0)

  <-- The entry went at once, with about 110 s of its hold time unused.
  <-- The two removal mechanisms are counted separately and correctly.  A TTL expiry in
      STEP 5 moved BOTH `Deleted Entries` and `Entry Age-outs`.  A shutdown frame moved
      `Deleted Entries` only, and `Entry Age-outs` stayed at 3.  So the DUT is not simply
      expiring the entry early.  It treats the shutdown frame as its own kind of event.
  <-- Frame accounting is still exact.  The In counter has now moved by exactly the
      number of frames sent at every step of this case: +3, +0, +3, +2, +1.
  => STEP 6 PASS: a TTL-0 LLDPDU deleted the neighbour entry immediately, and the device
     counted it as a delete and not as an age-out.

STEP 7 -- `lldp tlv-select` changes what is on the wire, and removal puts it back  PASS
